{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An invocation-specific plan binding one explicitly selected Product Definition and the complete companion population of one exact upstream release-matched cohort to owner-selected consumer bindings. Product Definition owns composition; the upstream cohort and each Product release record own release and member identity. Selection, plan and result are operation evidence, not persistent dependency declarations, registries or locks.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":368,
          "quote":"A Complete Consumer Update Plan binds one explicitly selected Product\nDefinition and the complete companion population of one exact upstream\nrelease-matched cohort to the consumer bindings selected by the\ninvocation's owner.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F4.%20Shared%20installed%20release%20basis%20and%20toolchain%20manager%2FExplicit%20complete%20consumer%20update",
          "start_line":361
        }
      ],
      "key":"complete_consumer_update_plan",
      "label":"Complete Consumer Update Plan"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The separate cohort-update operation presents an entire plan without consumer effects. Its acceptance object includes physical destinations and binding preimages, exact selected cohort bytes, companion annotated tags, commits, subtrees and inventories, replacement dependency locators, native routes and declared derived-source observations. Its complete mutation includes verification, immutable staging, preflight, rechecking, application and resulting-binding verification.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":380,
          "quote":"The separate `cohort-update` operation presents the whole plan without\nconsumer effects. Unlike the basis-only Adoption Plan above, its acceptance\nobject also binds the physical effect destinations and binding preimages,\nthe exact selected cohort bytes, the companion annotated tags, commits,\nsubtrees and inventories, the replacement dependency locators, the native\nroutes and the declared derived-source observations.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F4.%20Shared%20installed%20release%20basis%20and%20toolchain%20manager%2FExplicit%20complete%20consumer%20update",
          "start_line":375
        }
      ],
      "key":"complete_consumer_update_operation",
      "label":"Complete consumer update operation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Manager outcomes are predicate-specific refinements of the core result algebra, adding no core value. Effects answer whether the intended external effect occurred; admission and activation answer lawful admission on the exact subject and basis; construction availability answers whether a candidate is available. Non-effect commands evaluate their own subject predicates without an Effects value. Cause selects the result; effect value, established ending and partial occurrence are distinct coordinates.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":420,
          "quote":"Outcomes as results. The outcomes of the manager's operations in §4,\nits refusals, its reported completion and the recovery that\nloss requires, are stated in the core result algebra of\n[FOUNDATIONS §4 Results](FOUNDATIONS.md#results) through the refinements\nof [ACTIVATION §3](ACTIVATION.md#3-step-results-and-disposition), each\nfor its own predicate; the cause of an outcome selects its value, and the\nmapping adds no core value (P5, P6):",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F4.%20Shared%20installed%20release%20basis%20and%20toolchain%20manager%2FExplicit%20complete%20consumer%20update",
          "start_line":414
        }
      ],
      "key":"manager_result_domains",
      "label":"Manager result domains"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"An explicitly identified semantic scope whose governed vocabulary, concepts and relations are jointly interpretable under named authority. PRODUCT_DEFINITION declares ownership of urn:stdo:bounded-context:semantic-resolution under the selected complete STDO basis; a glossary can index but cannot declare this identity.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":501,
          "quote":"A bounded context is an explicitly identified semantic scope in\nwhich a governed vocabulary, concept set and relation set are jointly\ninterpretable under named authority.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":496
        }
      ],
      "key":"bounded_context",
      "label":"Bounded context"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A term is an occurrence-specific lexical label, not a context-free concept identity. Its semantic address is (term, bounded-context identity, owning authority, selected basis, governed scope). A concept reference is an exact resolvable locator of authority-owned meaning under a selected basis; it creates neither meaning nor authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":501,
          "quote":"A term is a lexical label used in an occurrence, not a context-free concept\nidentity.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":495
        },
        {
          "end_line":512,
          "quote":"(term, bounded-context identity, owning authority, selected basis, governed scope)",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":512
        }
      ],
      "key":"term_semantic_address",
      "label":"Term occurrence and semantic address"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"An explicit owner-authorised relation between exact concepts and bounded contexts. Semantic Import adopts unchanged meaning for a declared target scope. Semantic Translation changes meaning directionally and declares loss. Semantic Equivalence preserves distinct owners and contexts while claiming authority-established equality. Semantic Disambiguation selects one meaning for one scope without equating competitors. Specialisation is a directional translation preserving declared source meaning while narrowing admissible instances or adding target constraints.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":574,
          "quote":"A cross-context relation is explicit and owner-authorised.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":560
        },
        {
          "end_line":609,
          "quote":"A specialisation is a directional translation whose target preserves the\ndeclared source meaning while narrowing its admissible instances or adding\ntarget-context constraints.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":604
        }
      ],
      "key":"semantic_cross_context_relation",
      "label":"Cross-context semantic relation kinds"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A local axiom, override or disambiguation binding locates a source-owned local decision and its existing lawful authority and exact scope. Disambiguation metadata identifies the exact term, target context, complete material candidate references, selected candidate and all necessary semantic bases. The referenced URI owns the resolution; the overlay only makes the relation discoverable and checkable.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":641,
          "quote":"The referenced `uri` owns the resolution. When the candidates cross bounded\ncontexts, that carrier also declares the complete import, translation or\nequivalence relation §5 requires. The overlay metadata makes the relation\ndiscoverable and checkable; it neither restates the source-owned meanings\nnor mints the decision (P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F6.%20Local%20constitutional%20binding",
          "start_line":637
        }
      ],
      "key":"local_constitution_binding",
      "label":"Local constitutional binding"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The Product-owned accepted declaration of a shared frame set for finite actors collectively engaging one governed Product scope or outcome. It binds exact reference-frame law and optional profile basis; admitting authorities and scopes; frame declarations and evaluation inventory; actor-binding rules and capability envelopes; semantic, evaluation, operation and decision grants; result, conjunction, translation and coverage relations; activation triggers and invalidation law. reference_frame_bases is a non-empty locator set, not the declaration itself.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":662,
          "quote":"A Project Reference-Frame Basis is the Product-owned accepted declaration\nof the shared frame set through which finite actors collectively engage one\ngoverned Product scope or outcome",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F7.%20Collective%20reference-frame%20basis",
          "start_line":649
        }
      ],
      "key":"project_reference_frame_basis",
      "label":"Project Reference-Frame Basis"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"An execution-scoped binding of one agent to an applicable frame declaration or active configuration, exact evaluation, subject, basis, evidence boundary and capability envelope. A ticket or other authorised instruction carries the REFERENCE_FRAME §11 activation packet; the performed step is ACTIVATION §1. This temporary activation is distinct from the durable collective basis.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":704,
          "quote":"An Agent Frame\nActivation is the execution-scoped binding of one agent to an applicable\nframe declaration or active configuration, an exact evaluation, subject,\nbasis, evidence boundary and capability envelope;",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F7.%20Collective%20reference-frame%20basis",
          "start_line":695
        }
      ],
      "key":"agent_frame_activation",
      "label":"Agent Frame Activation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"PRODUCT_DEFINITION owns urn:stdo:bounded-context:build-tenancy under the selected complete STDO basis. Build tenancy is one constitutional WHAT realised by one or more independent project-owned HOW tenants; one tenant is the singleton case, and multiple independent realisations of the same WHAT are multi-build-tenancy. SPEC_METHOD §11 owns the tenant, the relation and tenant binding; TICKETS §4 owns the multi-build-tenant work-item relation. The Tenant Registry is canonical how.build_tenants; how.common binds realisation law explicitly adopted across more than one tenant.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":758,
          "quote":"In this context build tenancy is that one-or-more relation; one tenant is\nits singleton case, and multi-build-tenancy begins when the same WHAT has\nmore than one independent realisation",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F8.%20WHAT%2C%20HOW%20and%20work%20carriers",
          "start_line":755
        }
      ],
      "key":"build_tenancy_bindings",
      "label":"WHAT and HOW build-tenancy bindings"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"what.intent and what.product each locate one canonical current constitutional entrypoint; what.specification may locate multiple requirement or specification surfaces. ticketing.goals locates the current work wave, ticketing.tickets the durable ticket root and at least backlog, active and completed lanes, ticketing.comments the commentary root, and optional ticketing.sprints bounded execution-batch manifests. These bindings locate carrier kinds; SPEC_METHOD §4 and TICKETS determine which carrier a work item takes.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":784,
          "quote":"`ticketing.goals` locates the current work-wave carrier.\n`ticketing.tickets` locates the durable ticket root and the minimum\nbacklog, active and completed lanes. `ticketing.comments` locates the\ncommentary root. `ticketing.sprints` optionally locates bounded\nexecution-batch manifests.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F8.%20WHAT%2C%20HOW%20and%20work%20carriers",
          "start_line":775
        }
      ],
      "key":"constitutional_work_carriers",
      "label":"Constitutional and work carrier locators"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Recursive discovery finds stdo_*.json at the selected workspace root and arbitrarily deep nested project roots. Explicit composition entries relate otherwise independent definitions through a current target locator, expected stable product.definition_id, a directed relation authority specifying roles, owner, scope, lifecycle and invalidation, and one or more exact governing interface, capability, identity, lifecycle, data, evidence or other contracts.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":816,
          "quote":"Directory nesting creates no implicit inheritance, ownership, composition\nor constitutional override. Parent and child definitions stay independent\nuntil `composition` explicitly relates them.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F9.%20Recursive%20discovery%20and%20explicit%20composition",
          "start_line":803
        }
      ],
      "key":"definition_discovery_composition",
      "label":"Definition discovery and explicit composition"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A minimum conformance case set under an exact selected basis covers unique resolution and material collision. Without explicit cross-context relations: evaluation Frame and runtime Frame remain distinct; specialist-family Owner gains no semantic or decision-owner authority; immutable Product and mutable Product Definition remain distinct and are selected by occurrence context; build Tenant gives no default for hosted, customer, account, runtime or data tenancy; User remains unresolved among Product role, person, principal, account, actor, operator and data subject until downstream authority defines or explicitly relates applicable meanings.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":871,
          "quote":"| `User` | a Product role, human person, identity principal, account, actor, operator or data subject | unresolved until downstream authority defines or explicitly relates the applicable meanings |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F10.%20Semantic-resolution%20conformance%20cases",
          "start_line":871
        }
      ],
      "key":"semantic_resolution_cases",
      "label":"Semantic-resolution conformance cases"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"STDO-SURFACE-001 describes modular authorship with singular authority: each law has one owning member and other members consume it by reference. Members become operative only as one complete released method version. An exact cut identifies members, member digests, review basis, human acceptance and immutable identity. Compressions, templates, comments, candidate work, installed copies and mutable source are derived, evidential or authoring surfaces.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":895,
          "quote":"STDO-SURFACE-001. The shared methodology is modular in authorship and\nsingular in authority. Each law has one owning member; other members\nconsume it by reference. The members become operative only as one complete\nreleased version of the method",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F11.%20One%20constitutional%20surface%20and%20version%20boundary",
          "start_line":885
        }
      ],
      "key":"method_constitutional_surface",
      "label":"One constitutional surface and release boundary"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The per-product bootstrap begins from the exact selected cut's PRODUCT_DEFINITION_TEMPLATE.json, copied at the definition root as stdo_default.json for a singleton default or stdo_<label>.json for a named definition. Default carriers are the shared versioned store via constitution.stdo.basis; specification/GOALS.md, INTENT.md, PRODUCT.md and requirements/; build_tenants/common/ and default/; and .ai-workspace/sprints/, tickets/{backlog,active,completed}/ and comments/. These are starter defaults selected or replaced by URI bindings.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":952,
          "quote":"Every product defined under this method begins from\n`templates/PRODUCT_DEFINITION_TEMPLATE.json`, copied to the product's\ndefinition root as `stdo_default.json` for a singleton default project or\nas `stdo_<label>.json` for a named product definition.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F12.%20Bootstrap",
          "start_line":946
        }
      ],
      "key":"product_bootstrap",
      "label":"Product bootstrap and default scaffold"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A provenance and predecessor-disposition table claims conservation of overlay/layout independence (§1), required relations (§2), constitutional sufficiency (§3), shared installation/toolchain law (§4), complete update (§4 subsection), semantic resolution (§5), local binding (§6), collective frame basis (§7), WHAT/HOW/work carriers (§8), discovery/composition (§9), conformance cases (§10), constitutional version boundary (§11) and bootstrap (§12). It carries IDENTITY_METHOD→IDENTITY, RELEASE_METHOD→RELEASE, GLOSSARY_GUIDE→GLOSSARY, REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD→REFERENCE_FRAME and STDO_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASELINE→Executive baseline. It records ownership moves to PRODUCT_DEFINITION for routing, semantic resolution and build-tenancy contexts; design ontology wording is carried without claimed loss. Agent Frame Activation replaces the ambiguous phrase execution binding. Bootstrap generalises the predecessor's mutable source path. New material includes the §1 family table and cause-sensitive manager result mapping; semantic relation readings and unresolved-meaning result mapping are also identified. Routes are provenance, and carrying a renamed member is not itself a disposition.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1096,
          "quote":"Rows received by this member. Anchors are into rc.2 SPEC_METHOD.md; a\nlocus is a section of this file unless another member is named. Every row\nis restated in this member's own text; a route is provenance only. Where\nthe predecessor named one of its own members, the tenant member that\nreceives the same law is named instead; this is carrying, not a\ndisposition, and is recorded in the row.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F13.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line":1091
        }
      ],
      "key":"product_definition_source_dispositions",
      "label":"PRODUCT_DEFINITION predecessor disposition claims"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"PROFILES is a tier-3 member owning the core/profile boundary, declared-binding rule and profile import rule, and indexing profiles. A profile is a domain or operational specialisation; tiers 1 and 2 constitute the core.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":7,
          "quote":"Tier 3. Owns the boundary between the core and the profiles, the\ndeclared-binding rule and the import rule that every profile applies\n(§1), and indexes the profiles (§2 to §6). A profile is a domain or\noperational specialisation of the method; the members of tiers 1 and 2\nare the core.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PROFILES.md#PROFILES",
          "start_line":3
        }
      ],
      "key":"profiles_member",
      "label":"PROFILES member and core/profile categories"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A profile's declared binding maps a core mechanism to a domain or operational construct with direction, preserved meaning, narrowed scope and invalidation. This meaning of binding is distinct from Product adoption under PRODUCT_DEFINITION §7 and typed-contract carrier binding under UX §7. Profiles import the generic families they depend on and own only domain or operational additions. The declared-binding shape cites ODD specialisations of F_P and F_H as provenance; the import rule is new in this corpus.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":24,
          "quote":"A profile may bind a core mechanism to one of these;\nthe binding is declared in the profile with its direction, preserved\nmeaning, narrowed scope and invalidation.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PROFILES.md#PROFILES%2F1.%20The%20boundary",
          "start_line":18
        }
      ],
      "key":"profile_declared_binding",
      "label":"Profile declared binding and import boundary"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The graph-execution index names two tier-3 members. ODD receives all 48 ODD_METHOD rows, binds the bounded step to one vector or edge traversal, provides graph-native F_P/F_D/F_H shorthands with F_P/F_H specialisations of SPEC_METHOD §5, refines ACTIVATION §4 to graph functions and declares its cumulative environment contract for carrying earlier outputs. TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE receives 26 predecessor rows, 25 conserved and one proposed superseded pending the Product owner; it offers an optional closed model-family signature over one exact a_c basis for immutable occurrence histories over mutable subjects, with OP-001–OP-007. ODD §10 owns runtime/product/service ownership, ODD §5 keeps worker solution strategy outside the framework. This section indexes those bindings rather than owning them.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":71,
          "quote":"Where another member says that\nthis section keeps the ODD binding, the binding is the one ODD states and\nthis section indexes (P0, P1).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PROFILES.md#PROFILES%2F2.%20Graph%20execution",
          "start_line":69
        }
      ],
      "key":"graph_execution_profile_index",
      "label":"Graph execution profile index"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"DESIGN_MODULE is indexed as the software-design profile importing core law and binding ontology-first operation derivation through logical completeness, Prime contraction, irreducible architectural carriers and projections; proportional sequencing and the decision-complete gate; bounded frame conservation; native constructability; capability/foundation selection; transformation-admission completeness; invariant reconstruction; computational realisation projection; module taxonomy and realisation rules; review questions and checklist. ASSURANCE §§14–16 own the design-to-module evidence route, module-derived tests and lifecycle confirmation; SPEC_METHOD §2 owns core realisation shape and non-goals. DESIGN_MODULE §§5 and 7 decide per-boundary derivation cost.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":89,
          "quote":"[DESIGN_MODULE](DESIGN_MODULE.md) is the software-design profile.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PROFILES.md#PROFILES%2F3.%20Software%20design",
          "start_line":75
        }
      ],
      "key":"software_design_profile_index",
      "label":"Software-design profile index"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["For modules realising a UX surface."],
      "definition":"UX refines DESIGN_MODULE and imports the core. Indexed bindings include typed Elm State, Msg, Update, View, Cmd and Sub; message/product-carrier boundary; state transition law; effect membrane; AssetSurface or equivalent typed contract; continuation and Msg-replay proof; view shape; shared types with runtime validation at untrusted seams; accessibility minimum; realisation discretion; scaffold/exemption adoption and UX sprint compliance escrow; failure patterns/review questions; and result refinements each mapped to one core value. Its lead declares the binding of TICKETS §3 general escrow terms.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":104,
          "quote":"[UX](UX.md) is the UX profile. It refines DESIGN_MODULE for modules that\nrealise a UX surface, imports the core,",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PROFILES.md#PROFILES%2F4.%20UX",
          "start_line":93
        }
      ],
      "key":"ux_profile_index",
      "label":"UX profile index"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"WORLD_MODEL imports the core and binds product lineage and Builder Project specialisation; a candidate-status Markov-object construct with effective blanket, working refinements, epistemic status and promotion gate; claims and units; entity recovery and the published identity-bearing entity cut with admissibility, discrimination and publication law; attribute ledger; representation/materialisation/publication; overlay construction; saturation; local-artifact composition; projection; control/data planes; proof and world-model-driven development; and result refinements each mapped to one core value. Its lead declares the entity-cut binding of FOUNDATIONS §2 Object and P3's boundary claim.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":122,
          "quote":"[WORLD_MODEL](WORLD_MODEL.md) is the world-model profile. It imports the\ncore and binds: the imported product-lineage concepts and the Builder\nProject specialisation; the Markov-object construct at candidate status",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PROFILES.md#PROFILES%2F5.%20World%20model",
          "start_line":108
        }
      ],
      "key":"world_model_profile_index",
      "label":"World-model profile index"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"EXECUTIVE_BASELINE is an optional engagement-baseline profile importing core law and describing one executive configuration under ACTIVATION §1, with Executive, Worker and Reviewer engagement frames and a specialist-frame catalogue mapped onto tiers 1 and 2. Names bind responsibilities allocated by that configuration under Roles and delegation; spelling does not import roles.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":134,
          "quote":"[EXECUTIVE_BASELINE](EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md) is the optional\nengagement-baseline profile.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/PROFILES.md#PROFILES%2F6.%20Engagement%20baseline",
          "start_line":126
        }
